    "He calls it a 'mathematical trick' — but it is the price of admission to the quantum world",
    "Einstein — not for relativity but for this — takes the lumps literally in the photoelectric effect and wins the 1921 Nobel Prize"
  ],
  "why": "One constant, h = 6.626 × 10⁻³⁴ J·s, governs the granularity — and the classical continuum is what you see when the grains are too fine to notice."
};

const qfRedPredict = {
  "q": "Light below the threshold frequency shines on a metal. You crank the intensity up a thousandfold. What happens?",
  "choices": [
    "Electrons emerge after a delay, once they have slowly accumulated enough energy",
    "Still nothing — a trillion dim photons cannot stack, because they arrive one at a time",
    "Electrons emerge immediately, but with almost no kinetic energy",
    "The threshold frequency drops until electrons can escape"
  ],
  "answer": 1,
  "why": "Classical physics predicted delay and intensity-rescue; the experiments said the opposite. One photon, one electron — brightness controls only how many electrons emerge, never whether any do."
};

const qfPhotonEst = {
  "prompt": "Using the eV shortcut (photon energy ≈ 1240 / wavelength in nm): how many eV does one red 650 nm photon carry?",
  "unit": "eV",
  "answer": 1.9,
  "factor": 1.5,
  "why": "1240/650 ≈ 1.9 eV — short by 0.4 eV against sodium's 2.28 eV work function, so nothing happens regardless of brightness. Blue 450 nm carries 2.76 eV and ejects electrons at 0.48 eV.",
  "context": "The same shortcut runs semiconductors, solar cells and the spectroscopy lessons."
};

const qfSlitMatch = {
  "prompt": "One particle at a time — pair each fact with its meaning:",
  "pairs": [
    {
      "left": "λ = h/p (de Broglie, 1924)",
      "right": "every particle with momentum p has a wavelength — not a small one: a 50 eV electron's is ≈ 0.17 nm"
    },
    {
      "left": "electrons fired one at a time",
      "right": "each lands as a single dot, apparently random — yet thousands accumulate into fringes"
    },
    {
      "left": "buckyballs (C₆₀, 1999)",
      "right": "interference demonstrated for molecules, not just fundmental particles"
    },
    {
      "left": "detecting which slit the electron used",
      "right": "the fringes vanish; the pattern becomes two stripes"
    }
  ],
  "why": "Each particle explores both paths and the two amplitudes add — sometimes reinforcing, sometimes cancelling. Davisson and Germer confirmed the diffraction off nickel crystals in 1927."
};

const qfDetectorSpot = {
  "prompt": "One line of this student's double-slit lab notes is wrong — click it:",
  "lines": [
    "Fire the electrons one at a time: single dots, and the fringes build up dot by dot.",
    "De Broglie's λ = h/p fixes the electron's wavelength — about 0.17 nm at 50 eV.",
    "A gentler probe would keep the fringes intact, since it transfers less momentum to the electron.",
    "Any interaction that irreversibly records the path destroys the interference, however gentle.",
    "The two amplitudes must remain indistinguishable for interference to survive."
  ],
  "errorLine": 2,
  "correction": "Knowledge itself is not the mechanism and gentleness cannot save the fringes: any which-path record entangles the electron with the detector and decoheres the superposition — information, not force, does the destroying.",
  "why": "Classical observation means disturbance; quantum measurement-sensitivity is deeper — it is the theory's central postulate wearing experimental clothes."
};

const qfEnergyTimeJudge = {
  "statements": [
    {
      "s": "ΔE · Δt ≥ ħ/2 has a measurement-disturbance reading, just like Δx · Δp.",
      "truth": false,
      "why": "It has no measurement-disturbance reading at all — it says short-lived states have uncertain energy."
    },
    {
      "s": "An excited atom decaying after ~10 ns has a spectral line broadened by about 10⁻⁷ eV.",
      "truth": true,
      "why": "The natural linewidth — the fact spectroscopists exploit to measure lifetimes."
    },
    {
      "s": "Virtual particles borrow energy for a time the inequality permits.",
      "truth": true,
      "why": "The accounting that makes quantum field theory work."
    },
    {
      "s": "Better instruments could beat the Δx · Δp limit.",
      "truth": false,
      "why": "The disturbance story would predict that, and they cannot — it is Fourier mathematics: localising a wave forces momentum spread."
    }
  ]
};

const qfTwoTierBranch = {
  "root": "An electron flies toward a screen, unmeasured. Walk the two-tier structure of quantum prediction.",
  "nodes": {
    "start": {
      "text": "While it flies, what is the electron's story?",
      "choices": [
        {
          "label": "A wave evolving smoothly and deterministically under the Schrödinger equation",
          "next": "dot"
        },
        {
          "label": "A dot already waiting at its landing spot",
          "next": "nopreset"
        }
      ]
    },
    "nopreset": {
      "text": "That is the picture the theory refuses to give: no equation governs which outcome occurs — only the statistics.",
      "choices": [
        {
          "label": "So the dot appears — what is predicted?",
          "next": "dot"
        }
      ]
    },
    "dot": {
      "text": "The dot appears. What does the theory predict about this individual event?",
      "choices": [
        {
          "label": "Exactly where it lands, if the wavefunction is known well enough",
          "next": "refusal"
        },
        {
          "label": "Nothing individually — only Born-rule probabilities over many runs",
          "next": "statistics"
        }
      ]
    },
    "refusal": {
      "text": "That is the refusal: individual events are unpredictable. The electron's magnetic moment matches theory to one part in a trillion — in the statistics.",
      "choices": [
        {
          "label": "Continue",
          "next": "statistics"
        }
      ]
    },
    "statistics": {
      "text": "Between measurements the wave evolves smoothly again — deterministic evolution, then a probabilistic jump no equation governs. Whether that jump is a complete description of nature is the measurement problem, the page's open wound.",
      "choices": []
    }
  },
  "why": "Quantum mechanics predicts the statistics of outcomes with spectacular precision while declining to predict individual events — the two-tier structure is the theory's shape."
};

const qfPsiTrace = {
  "prompt": "An electron trapped in a box from x = 0 to 1. Which curve is its ground-state probability density |ψ|²?",
  "curves": [
    {
      "expr": "sin(3.1416*x)^2",
      "label": "sin²(πx)",
      "color": "#1d4ed8"
    },
    {
      "expr": "sin(3.1416*x)",
      "label": "sin(πx) — the wavefunction itself",
      "color": "#b42318"
    },
    {
      "expr": "sin(6.283*x)^2",
      "label": "sin²(2πx) — the first excited state",
      "color": "#0f766e"
    }
  ],
  "correct": 0,
  "x": {
    "min": 0,
    "max": 1
  },
  "y": {
    "min": -0.1,
    "max": 1.2
  },
  "xlabel": "position in the box",
  "ylabel": "probability density",
  "why": "The Born rule: probability density is the squared magnitude — no sign, peak at the centre, zero at the walls. A standing wave in a box plays discrete notes, which is what makes orbitals discrete."
};
---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/physics/advanced/quantum-foundations">
  <h2>Where you are, and what you will learn</h2>
  <p>
    This is an advanced lesson in Physics. It assumes the foundational lessons <em>Energy
    Conservation</em> and <em>Motion in One Dimension</em>, and the intermediate lessons
    <em>Waves and Sound</em> and <em>Electrostatics</em>, which supply the wave behaviour and
    the electrical attraction this chapter bends into something new.
  </p>
  <p>By the end you will be able to:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>read the photoelectric plot: threshold frequency, work function, and why intensity cannot rescue a too-red photon;</li>
    <li>explain self-interference in the double slit, and what a which-path record does to the fringes;</li>
    <li>state the uncertainty principle as wave mathematics, not measurement clumsiness — and compute the confinement cost;</li>
    <li>apply the Born rule and say where the theory is deterministic and where it refuses to be;</li>
    <li>describe what Bell-test experiments ruled out, and what they left standing.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    By 1900, classical physics was complete and, physicists assumed, finished. Then the
    seams split. The first: <strong>blackbody radiation</strong>. Classical
    electromagnetism predicted any warm object should radiate infinite energy at short
    wavelengths (the "ultraviolet catastrophe") — every oven a death ray. Planck fixed the
    arithmetic in 1900 with a reluctant assumption: matter exchanges energy with light in
    discrete lumps of size <strong>E = hf</strong>, where h = 6.626 × 10⁻³⁴ J·s. He called
    it a "mathematical trick"; it was the price of admission to the quantum world. The
    second seam was the photoelectric effect, and Einstein — not for relativity but for
    this — took the lumps literally and won the 1921 Nobel Prize.
  </p>
  <Widget no="3" caption="rebuild the two seams that split classical physics" widget="order" config={qfSeamsOrder} />
  <p>
    The pattern of the whole subject is already visible in those two cracks: nature is
    <strong>granular</strong> at small scales, the granularity is governed by one constant
    h, and the classical continuum is what you see when the grains are too fine to notice.
    This page builds the modern framework — wavefunctions, probability, uncertainty,
    superposition — and ends at the question nobody has closed: what, exactly, happens
    during a measurement.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the photoelectric plot — the crossing marks the threshold frequency; below it, nothing happens, ever" widget="plotter" config={photoCfg} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> the blue line is the maximum electron energy for sodium
    against light frequency, and its crossing with the zero line is the threshold — about
    5.5 × 10¹⁴ Hz. Left of the crossing the formula gives negative energy — physics' way of
    saying no electrons emerge <em>ever</em>, however bright the light. The straightness is the
    point — constant slope, one slope for every metal: Planck's h measured in the laboratory.
  </p>

  <h2>The photoelectric effect: light as particles</h2>
  <p>
    Shine light on a metal; electrons come out. Classical physics predicts any colour
    should work given enough intensity (more amplitude, more energy to shake electrons
    loose) and predicts a delay while the electron slowly accumulates energy. The
    experiments (Lenard, 1902; Millikan's precise tests) said the opposite: below a
    <strong>threshold frequency</strong> no electrons emerge <em>ever</em>, at any
    intensity; above it, ejection is immediate even in faint light; and the electrons'
    maximum kinetic energy depends only on frequency, rising one volt per doubling of
    frequency — with brightness controlling only <em>how many</em> electrons emerge.
    Einstein's explanation: light arrives in quanta (photons) of energy hf. One photon,
    one electron; the photon must deliver at least the <strong>work function</strong> φ
    (the energy binding the electron to that metal), and the remainder is kinetic:
    <strong>KE<sub>max</sub> = hf − φ</strong> — the straight line in the figure, slope h.
  </p>
  <Widget no="4" caption="bet before you read on — the too-red lamp" widget="predict" config={qfRedPredict} />
  <p>
    Worked numbers for sodium (φ = 2.28 eV): the threshold is f = φ/h = 2.28 / (4.136 ×
    10⁻¹⁵ eV·s) ≈ 5.5 × 10¹⁴ Hz — green light, wavelength about 545 nm. Red light at
    650 nm carries photon energy 1240/650 ≈ 1.9 eV: short by 0.4 eV, so <em>nothing</em>
    happens regardless of brightness — a trillion dim photons cannot stack, because they
    arrive one at a time. Blue 450 nm light carries 2.76 eV, ejecting electrons at
    0.48 eV. The shortcut "photon energy in eV ≈ 1240 divided by wavelength in nm" comes
    straight from hc and is worth remembering; it runs the whole business of
    semiconductors, solar cells and the spectroscopy lessons in chemistry.
  </p>
  <Widget no="5" caption="guess the magnitude — a photon's budget in eV" widget="estimate" config={qfPhotonEst} />
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> light delivers energy in lumps of hf — granular, with a threshold
    no intensity can cross. Next, the experiment that forces those lumps to behave like waves:
    the double slit, one particle at a time.
  </p>

  <h2>The double slit: each particle interferes with itself</h2>
  <p>
    If light is particles, beams through two slits should paint two stripes. They paint
    <strong>interference fringes</strong> — and so do electrons, neutrons, atoms, and
    molecules as large as buckyballs (C₆₀ in 1999; larger molecules since). Fire electrons <em>one at a time</em>,
    so no two can interact: each lands as a single dot, apparently random; accumulate
    thousands and the fringes emerge dot by dot. Each electron somehow explores
    <em>both</em> paths, and the two amplitudes add — sometimes reinforcing, sometimes
    cancelling. De Broglie (1924) supplied the wave's wavelength:
    <strong>λ = h/p</strong> — every particle with momentum p has a wavelength. It is not
    small: a 50 eV electron has λ ≈ 1.2/√50 ≈ 0.17 nm (the formula λ(nm) = 1.226/√V),
    electron-microscope scale, and Davisson and Germer confirmed electron diffraction off
    nickel crystals in 1927. But detect the electron at one slit — learn <em>which path
    it took</em> — and the fringes vanish; the pattern becomes two stripes. Knowledge
    itself is not the mechanism: any interaction that irreversibly records the path does
    the destroying. This measurement-sensitivity is not a defect. It is the theory's
    central postulate wearing experimental clothes.
  </p>
  <Widget no="6" caption="pair each double-slit fact with its meaning" widget="match" config={qfSlitMatch} />
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "a gentler which-path detector would keep the fringes —
  the photons needed to detect the electron just knock it about." It seems reasonable because
  classical observation means disturbance, and gentler probes disturb less. But knowledge
  itself is not the mechanism: <em>any</em> interaction that irreversibly records the path
  destroys the fringes, even one transferring negligible momentum. The correct picture:
  interference requires the two amplitudes to remain indistinguishable; a which-path record —
  however gentle — entangles the electron with the detector and decoheres the superposition.
  Information, not force, does the destroying.</p>
  <Widget no="7" caption="find the faulty line in this student's lab notes" widget="spot-error" config={qfDetectorSpot} />
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> each particle explores both paths and the amplitudes add — and any
    irreversible path record collapses the pattern to two stripes. Next, the principle that
    makes this unavoidable: localisation itself, not instrumentation, sets the limits.
  </p>

  <h2>The uncertainty principle: a fact about waves, not about clumsiness</h2>
  <p>
    Heisenberg (1927): <strong>Δx · Δp ≥ ħ/2</strong>, where ħ = h/2π. The usual pop
    version — "measuring disturbs the system" — undersells it badly; the disturbance
    story would predict better instruments could beat the limit, and they cannot. The
    truth is Fourier mathematics: any wave localised in a narrow region <em>must</em> be
    built from many wavelengths (a single wavelength is a wave spread over all space),
    and since momentum is wavelength via de Broglie, localising position forces momentum
    spread. Position and momentum are like pitch and duration of a musical note: a
    0.01-second blip genuinely has no single pitch. The pairs are <strong>conjugate</strong>
    variables, and no state of the universe ever has both sharply defined.
  </p>

  <Widget no="2" caption="the payoff — confinement costs energy, and that is why atoms have the size they have" widget="equationStep" config={uncCfg} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> step through the arithmetic and watch the squeeze backfire
    — tighter confinement forces momentum spread, and the kinetic energy climbs as 1/L²: ten
    times tighter costs a hundred times more. The final step is the punchline: balance that
    confinement cost against electrostatic attraction and an equilibrium size falls out near
    0.1 nm. The uncertainty principle is not a footnote; it fixes the size of every atom you
    have touched.
  </p>

  <p>
    The energy–time cousin, <strong>ΔE · Δt ≥ ħ/2</strong>, has no measurement-disturbance
    reading at all: it says short-lived states have uncertain energy. Excited atoms that
    decay after ~10 ns have spectral lines broadened by about 10⁻⁷ eV (natural linewidth
    — the fact spectroscopists exploit to measure lifetimes); virtual particles borrow
    energy for a time permitted by this inequality, the accounting that makes quantum
    field theory work.
  </p>
  <Widget no="8" caption="true or false — the energy–time cousin" widget="judge" config={qfEnergyTimeJudge} />
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> uncertainty is wave mathematics — localising a wave forces
    momentum spread, and confinement visibly costs energy. Next, the object that carries all
    of this: the wavefunction, and the two-tier rule for measurement.
  </p>

  <h2>The wavefunction: probability as physics</h2>
  <p>
    The modern picture (Schrödinger, 1926): every particle is described by a
    <strong>wavefunction</strong> ψ, a wave over configuration space whose squared
    magnitude |ψ|² gives the probability density of finding the particle at each place
    (the Born rule). The wavefunction evolves deterministically under the Schrödinger
    equation — the quantum analogue of F = ma, and the differential-equations lesson's
    craft applies to it directly. Then, upon measurement, one outcome occurs with the
    Born-rule probability, and the wavefunction is taken to "collapse" to the observed
    branch. Notice the two-tier structure: between measurements, smooth wave evolution;
    at measurement, a probabilistic jump no equation governs. Quantum mechanics predicts
    the <em>statistics</em> of outcomes with spectacular precision — the electron's
    magnetic moment matches theory to one part in a trillion, the best-verified number in
    science — while declining to predict individual events. Whether that refusal is a
    complete description of nature is the measurement problem, this page's open wound.
  </p>
  <Widget no="9" caption="walk the two-tier rule for measurement" widget="branch" config={qfTwoTierBranch} />

  <h2>Quantised atoms: standing waves make the periodic table</h2>
  <p>
    Confinement quantises. Trap any wave in a box and only standing-wave patterns
    survive — a guitar string plays discrete notes, not every frequency. Electrons in
    atoms are waves trapped by nuclear attraction, so only discrete <strong>orbitals</strong>
    exist, each with a definite energy. Bohr's model (1913) got hydrogen right for
    semi-accidental reasons (his condition 2πr = nλ is exactly the standing-wave
    requirement); the correct energies are E<sub>n</sub> = −13.6/n² eV: −13.6, −3.4,
    −1.51 eV for n = 1, 2, 3. Photons are emitted or absorbed only at the
    <em>differences</em>: the n = 3 → 2 transition emits 1.89 eV — wavelength
    1240/1.89 ≈ 656 nm, the red hydrogen-alpha line that stains every emission nebula in
    the astronomy images in this atlas. Each element's orbital ladder is unique, so each
    element's spectrum is a fingerprint — the tool that found helium in the Sun before it
    was found on Earth, and that powers the spectroscopy and astronomy pages. Chemistry's
    whole architecture (shells, valence, the periodic table's shape) is this quantisation
    plus the Pauli exclusion principle, which forbids two electrons sharing one quantum
    state and so forces the table's structure rather than a collapse of all atoms into
    their ground states.
  </p>
  <Widget no="10" caption="which curve is the ground-state probability cloud?" widget="trace" config={qfPsiTrace} />
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "electrons orbit the nucleus like planets — miniature
  spheres circling at fixed distances." It seems reasonable because the word "orbital" and
  Bohr's planetary sketch invite it. But the electron is a standing wave trapped by nuclear
  attraction: only discrete orbitals exist, each a probability cloud in |ψ|², not a track —
  the n = 1, 2, 3 energies are the standing-wave ladder. The correct picture: ask "where
  might I find the electron" (the Born rule), never "where is it moving"; and the Pauli
  exclusion principle fills the rungs in the order that builds the periodic table rather than
  letting every atom collapse into its ground state.</p>

  <h2>Entanglement: the part of quantum theory that changed philosophy</h2>
  <p>
    Two particles can be prepared in a joint state — say, total spin zero — so that
    measuring one instantly fixes what the other <em>must</em> yield when measured,
    however far away. Einstein, Podolsky and Rosen (1935) argued this "spooky action at a
    distance" meant quantum mechanics was incomplete: surely the particles carried
    pre-agreed instructions (hidden variables) all along. In 1964 John Bell proved the
    question could be decided by experiment: any hidden-variable theory of the agreed
    type implies correlations bounded by a specific number; quantum mechanics predicts
    stronger ones. Experiments — Freedman–Clauser (1972), Aspect (1982), and the
    loophole-free tests of 2015 that earned the 2022 Nobel prize — found the quantum
    correlations, decisively. The lesson is subtle and often garbled: entanglement
    correlates outcomes but <strong>transmits no signal</strong> — each side's local
    results are random no matter what the other does, so relativity's no-faster-than-light
    causality survives intact. What dies is the assumption that distant particles carry
    definite local properties before measurement — Bell's own term for such properties,
    "local beables", became the literature's label for exactly what the experiments rule
    out.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> quantum "observation" needs a conscious observer.
    Nothing in the formalism mentions minds. What destroys interference is
    <strong>decoherence</strong> — irreversible entanglement with the environment (a
    stray photon, an air molecule, a detector's amplifier), which happens staggering
    fast for macroscopic objects and is why tables have definite positions while
    electrons in carefully isolated traps do not. Consciousness entered the conversation
    through von Neumann's 1932 book and never left the bestseller shelf; it plays no role
    in any working prediction.
  </p>

  <h2>Check yourself</h2>
  <p>Try these before moving on. Feedback follows each answer.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>State what KE<sub>max</sub> = hf − φ predicts, and explain why raising the light's intensity leaves KE<sub>max</sub> unchanged.</li>
    <li>Sodium's work function is 2.28 eV. A 450 nm photon arrives (energy 2.76 eV). Compute the maximum kinetic energy of the ejected electron.</li>
    <li>Why does squeezing an electron into a region ten times smaller raise its kinetic energy by roughly a factor of a hundred?</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    <strong>Feedback:</strong> (1) One photon, one electron: the photon's hf pays the work
    function φ and the remainder is kinetic — intensity changes how many electrons emerge,
    never each one's energy budget. (2) KE = hf − φ = 2.76 − 2.28 = 0.48 eV (the lesson's
    worked number; shortcut check: 1240/450 ≈ 2.76 eV). (3) The uncertainty principle:
    Δp ≥ ħ/2Δx, so ten times tighter confinement means ten times the momentum spread, and
    KE ≈ (Δp)²/2m scales with its square — a hundredfold.
  </p>

  <h2>Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Energy is exchanged in lumps of E = hf; the photoelectric plot's threshold and slope show it directly.</li>
    <li>Each particle interferes with itself in the double slit; any irreversible which-path record destroys the fringes.</li>
    <li>Δx · Δp ≥ ħ/2 is wave mathematics, not measurement clumsiness — and confinement visibly costs energy.</li>
    <li>The wavefunction evolves deterministically between measurements; outcomes follow the Born rule, individually unpredictable.</li>
    <li>Orbitals are standing-wave probability ladders; with Pauli exclusion they build the periodic table.</li>
    <li>Bell tests rule out local hidden variables; entanglement correlates outcomes but transmits no signal.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where this goes next</h2>
  <p>
    The natural next steps in this subject are <em>Special Relativity</em> — the century's other
    revolution, met here already in Einstein's photoelectric Nobel — and <em>Statistical
    Mechanics</em>, which builds macroscopic thermodynamics from microscopic quantum counting.
    The expert lesson <em>Quantum Field Theory</em> upgrades the stage to fields, as sketched in
    the sections above.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>The formal skeleton.</strong> States are vectors in a complex vector space
    (Hilbert space); observables are Hermitian operators on them; possible measurement
    outcomes are the operators' eigenvalues — quantisation is the spectral theorem, the
    same eigenvalue mathematics as the matrix lesson's eigenvectors, now with physical
    meaning. Incompatible observables correspond to non-commuting operators: the
    commutator [x̂, p̂] = iħ <em>is</em> the uncertainty principle, stated in algebra.
    Superposition is just vector addition — the "both paths" of the double slit is no
    more mysterious than a chord being two notes at once; the mystery is only why
    <em>measurements</em> refuse to superpose. The expert-tier quantum field theory lesson
    upgrades the stage: particles become excitations of fields, and photon number itself
    becomes a quantum variable.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Tunneling: the wavefunction pays no rent to walls.</strong> Where the
    potential energy exceeds the particle's energy, the wavefunction does not stop — it
    decays exponentially, and if the barrier is thin enough a tail survives on the far
    side: the particle sometimes appears where Newton forbids. This is not exotic: the
    Sun shines only because protons tunnel through their electrostatic repulsion at
    15-million-degree temperatures (classically, fusion would need a hundred times
    hotter); alpha decay <em>is</em> tunneling, with lifetimes from nanoseconds to 10²¹
    years explained by one barrier width; scanning tunneling microscopes image individual
    atoms on exactly this current; and flash memory writes bits by pushing electrons
    through oxide barriers billions of times a day.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Interpretations: same predictions, different worlds.</strong> The formalism
    above is shared; the fight is over what it <em>means</em>. The Copenhagen view treats
    collapse as fundamental and questions about "what was really there" as meaningless.
    The many-worlds view (Everett, 1957) removes collapse: the wavefunction never
    breaks, and every branching superposition is real — measurement is just the observer
    becoming entangled. De Broglie–Bohm pilot-wave theory keeps particles with definite
    trajectories guided by the wave — non-local, but fully deterministic. Objective-
    collapse theories (GRW) modify the Schrödinger equation itself and are — uniquely —
    experimentally testable; current data constrain them. No interpretation yet predicts
    anything different, which is precisely why the debate has run for a century: the
    measurement problem is not a puzzle to be solved but a choice among unfinished
    stories.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Open frontier.</strong> The no-cloning theorem (an unknown quantum state
    cannot be copied) makes quantum money and quantum key distribution possible in
    principle and quantum computers — which compute in superposition and are Bell's
    correlations put to work — an engineering programme rather than a physics doubt; the
    open questions are error rates and scale, not foundations. Deeper: whether gravity
    must be quantised (almost certainly yes — but no direct quantum-gravitational
    measurement exists), whether collapse models will finally break the degeneracy
    between interpretations, and whether spacetime itself is emergent from entanglement
    ("it from qubit") are the live research questions. The foundations laid here —
    amplitude, superposition, the Born rule, conjugate uncertainty — are, a century on,
    both the settled core and the scene of the crime.
  </p>

